1
00:00:00,280 --> 00:00:02,040
I'm Yoko Hikasa. I'm Ayane Sakura.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:08,340
“You're wearing them!” “You said you were done putting them on.”

3
00:00:08,340 --> 00:00:12,800
“It's fine. We can talk without earrings.”

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:16,620
“Then take them off! Don't wear them!”

5
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:23,880
Hiyo's earrings were cute, so I wanted some too. That's why I put these on.

6
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:31,700
“This is bad.” “What's bad?” “They're from when you were with your old agency.”

7
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:38,180
“What's wrong with that? I left on good terms with the agency.”

8
00:00:38,180 --> 00:00:44,160
“What about publicity rights?” “Earrings have publicity rights? They belonged to Hiyo anyway.”

9
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:51,300
“My face belongs to me too. Is this a rights issue?” “It's fine. You can't wear them now?”

10
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:59,780
Why take them off and put them back on during the show? We're free to do that on the radio.

11
00:00:59,780 --> 00:01:09,880
We can talk without earrings. There's a mirror here, so put them on. Anyway, last week was full of sound effects.

12
00:01:09,880 --> 00:01:16,780
We're very sorry. “A note saying ‘Don't talk to Maaya Uchida’ showed up?”

13
00:01:16,780 --> 00:01:24,680
It was handwritten on a tissue. “So Maaya was in this studio?” “Never mind that.”

14
00:01:24,680 --> 00:01:32,460
“Does the studio smell like Maaya? I want to see her.” “I haven't seen her in ages.”

15
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:39,100
“Didn't you meet Akari Kito?” “That was just yesterday, actually.”

16
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:48,100
It was near the end of this past week—yesterday, the day we recorded.

17
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:55,900
“That was already in the script? That's creepy. How did you know I met Kito?”

18
00:01:55,900 --> 00:02:04,020
“There's a spy on this show!” “I can't say anything here.” “It's Spy Classroom.”

19
00:02:04,020 --> 00:02:13,680
“Kito isn't on that show.” “Not yet, but she's coming on soon. We got a tip.”

20
00:02:13,680 --> 00:02:22,560
“Do you meet every week, or was it a one-off?” “We don't meet every week; it was a coincidence.”

21
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:30,520
“What did you talk about?” “I greeted her with ‘Hey, bean sprout!’ instead of ‘Good morning.’”

22
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:40,520
“That's laying it on thick as a senior.” “What did she say?” “‘I'm not a bean sprout.’”

23
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:55,360
“She's a funny one.” “I can hear her saying that.” “She really would.”

24
00:02:55,360 --> 00:03:05,700
Then I told her about the prank Saku and I had been planning.

25
00:03:05,700 --> 00:03:14,320
“Why tell her before you've even pulled it off?” “We kept saying we might not do it.”

26
00:03:14,320 --> 00:03:25,720
It was a “preview prank.” I thought she'd feel bad if she got genuinely upset.

27
00:03:25,880 --> 00:03:36,800
“She has nerves of steel.” “Is she really that tough?” “She's weaker than me, but tougher than most.”

28
00:03:36,800 --> 00:03:47,900
“Who's tougher, you or Hiyo?” “Hiyo's mental strength is hard to pin down.”

29
00:03:47,900 --> 00:04:03,360
Before Hiyo arrived, we were wondering whether Yoko Hikasa is mentally tough or fragile.

30
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:12,800
We were asking whether she ever has a day when she isn't cheerful. “Today, actually.”

31
00:04:12,800 --> 00:04:23,320
“You came in without saying anything.” “The door said a recording was in progress.”

32
00:04:23,320 --> 00:04:36,160
I opened it and found Saku and Chanko talking about the dark side of the industry in a pitch-black room.

33
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:45,720
Our high-school-girl writer had thoughtfully closed the door. “That sounds like a serious conversation.”

34
00:04:45,720 --> 00:04:54,580
“Stop nodding like that! What was it?” “I'll tell you as soon as we cut.”

35
00:04:54,580 --> 00:05:08,480
I've known Hiyo for over ten years, and I've hardly ever seen her without energy. She always perks up around people.

36
00:05:08,480 --> 00:05:18,520
“So how low does she get at home?” “I don't really get down. I'm more just expressionless.”

37
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:27,880
Even when she drinks water, she just has a straight face.

38
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:41,240
“Maybe you're all feeling gloomy after that dark conversation.” “You're right.”

39
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:51,540
“Hiyo, shine your sunshine on us.” “You're wearing orange today, after all.”

40
00:05:51,540 --> 00:06:04,840
“I'm shrouded in darkness.” “Wear something brighter next time. Last week you looked like the sky.”

41
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:15,760
“Today it's darkness and something growing in a gutter.” “Did you just call my outfit gutter-colored?”

42
00:06:15,760 --> 00:06:30,820
“It's pretty blue-gray lace!” “On camera it looks like a gutter.” “Apologize to my expensive clothes!”

43
00:06:30,820 --> 00:06:42,060
“This is going on YouTube!” “Can you lower the video quality?”

44
00:06:42,060 --> 00:06:55,040
When the first video went up, our LINE group was freaking out about how sharp the YouTube picture was.

45
00:06:55,040 --> 00:07:06,580
“You were bare-faced!” “I let my guard down and came without makeup the second time.”

46
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:15,800
“What happens to the second episode on YouTube?” “We'll lower the quality.”

47
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:29,919
You two were discussing video quality and Twitter icons with all these technical terms flying around.

48
00:07:29,919 --> 00:07:43,759
I couldn't tell whether any of it was good or bad. Every time you got technical, I was told to sit there quietly.

49
00:07:43,759 --> 00:07:59,020
I started to panic, wondering if I should do something. Then I checked out and fell asleep.

50
00:07:59,020 --> 00:08:10,540
“But you installed radiko?” “I did. I caught the second episode live.”

51
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:22,420
On the app, there's that bar you can drag back and forth. How do I rewind a little?

52
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:29,440
“Dragging it with your finger doesn't go back.” “That might be hard during a live broadcast.”

53
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:40,300
“Fix it for me.” “I can't. That's radiko's job, not Producer Iguchi's.”

54
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:51,880
After listening live at that hour, I realized we might be a little loud.

55
00:08:52,340 --> 00:09:03,740
“A bit loud for one in the morning?” “I listened to the archive, and your voices sound alike.”

56
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:14,640
When it's audio only, I sometimes can't tell which one of us is talking. We even have the same chime sound.

57
00:09:14,640 --> 00:09:24,860
We should've used different chimes—red and green, maybe. “Synesthesia?” “Exactly.”

58
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:38,860
Maybe we need to make our characters more distinct. Hiyo, could you use your usual big, loud voice?

59
00:09:40,860 --> 00:09:52,860
“Should I raise my pitch?” “Wouldn't that make you even louder?”

60
00:09:52,860 --> 00:10:05,860
Maybe it's just our laughter that sounds alike. Our laughing fits together perfectly.

61
00:10:05,860 --> 00:10:15,420
My mom—Shinobu—gave me her thoughts on the first episode. “Don't say her name!”

62
00:10:15,420 --> 00:10:25,940
But she hasn't said anything about the second one. Maybe we said “ding” too many times.

63
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:41,200
She loves Hiyo, though. After the first episode she said it was funny and praised Hiyo's steady presence.

64
00:10:41,200 --> 00:10:57,360
“She stopped liking you pretty quickly.” “What was that ‘Sokkuri Blue’ thing you said last week?”

65
00:10:57,360 --> 00:11:11,360
I said it on impulse, then felt differently the next morning. I left the studio laughing, “It's fine, it's fine!”

66
00:11:11,360 --> 00:11:23,360
Then I thought, “Maybe that wasn't great.” It got summed up as “women voice actors and …”

67
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:35,360
“I'm glad they kept that in.” “From now on, add a chime whenever I say something like that.”

68
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:47,480
“That'll make more work!” They even considered adding the chime for only one of us.

69
00:11:47,480 --> 00:12:03,240
The agency wanted to be careful: add it for Saku but not Hiyo, or the other way around.

70
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:19,040
“Why not add it for both?” “I made sure they got that message.”

71
00:12:19,040 --> 00:12:29,880
“Who says the most dirty jokes on Bunka Hōsō?” “As long as Onozaka-san is around, we're fine.”

72
00:12:29,880 --> 00:12:42,960
“And Tomokazu Seki-san.” “Wow. But that was a different era.”

73
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:55,360
“Seki-san used to eat lotion.” “Don't bring that up—you'll be sad about Lotion Blue tomorrow!”

74
00:12:55,360 --> 00:13:07,660
Hiyo suddenly started making strange sounds. Chanko was holding her head, wondering what was happening.

75
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:19,000
Sometimes when Hiyo gets stressed, she suddenly gets silly to balance herself out.

76
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:30,840
“That sudden ‘I'm having fun now!’ is a thing.” “Maybe it's how I make everything go blank.”

77
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:44,600
I just thought of a favorite Hiyo story, but I absolutely can't tell it.

78
00:13:44,600 --> 00:13:56,220
“You like it but can't tell us?” “I'll try. If it's not okay, add a sound effect.”

79
00:13:56,220 --> 00:14:11,100
“A certain senior…” “Nope. Sorry. Please forget that.”

80
00:14:11,100 --> 00:14:18,720
All right, let's begin. Please give us the title call.

81
00:14:21,900 --> 00:14:37,280
This is “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions,” a variety talk show for two people who could chat for hours.

82
00:14:37,280 --> 00:14:44,520
We keep our digressions to exactly 30 minutes. Follow the show's official X account and use #Yodan.

83
00:14:44,520 --> 00:14:52,800
“This way, senior!” “Don't say that!” “That story was funny, though.”

84
00:14:52,800 --> 00:15:08,740
I heard that a senior who'd been really down felt much better after something I said. “I said something?”

85
00:15:08,740 --> 00:15:19,020
“Your words turned the whole room around.” “You could've been a little more vague.” “I didn't need to say everything?”

86
00:15:19,020 --> 00:15:30,480
“I'm still learning how to do radio. I'm 24, after all.” “That character setting is still going?”

87
00:15:30,480 --> 00:15:41,200
“I haven't forgotten it since episode one.” “You've really kept it going!”

88
00:15:41,200 --> 00:15:52,260
Apparently people were posting the phrase under the hashtag. “Nobody's doing it!” “Anyway, any digressions?”

89
00:15:52,260 --> 00:16:05,400
“We really should finish in 30 minutes. When we go ten minutes over, you can feel the editing.” “That's a real reflection.”

90
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:19,680
Last week they had to cut us off. Hiyo was the main culprit—she ignored the wrap-up cue and said something she shouldn't.

91
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:35,920
“Anyway, since this is a digression…” “What are you doing? Are you trying to get me drunk?”

92
00:16:35,920 --> 00:16:48,960
“You're such a troublesome junior.” “This is a current show, right?” “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions!”

93
00:16:49,060 --> 00:17:04,980
I never notice Director Yama-chan giving us the cue. To me, he's like a fairy.

94
00:17:04,980 --> 00:17:19,560
I've talked to him, but I don't know his voice. It's like he's speaking directly into my brain by telepathy.

95
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:32,200
“Then Chanko can give the cue.” “Let's try it.” “What was that just now?”

96
00:17:32,200 --> 00:17:46,420
“Only Hiyo enjoyed that. The adults are being led around by her.” “A funny story? No—a funny person.”

97
00:17:46,420 --> 00:18:05,060
When I was turning left at a crosswalk, an older woman suddenly made a strange face and her knees buckled.

98
00:18:05,060 --> 00:18:18,820
I thought she might fall after crossing, so I got ready to get out and help. But she just kept walking.

99
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:29,820
I was worried she'd crouch down after crossing, but she walked right on. “That's a digression!”

100
00:18:31,860 --> 00:18:44,880
I yelled “Whoa!” to myself in the car. I'm glad nothing happened, though.

101
00:18:44,880 --> 00:19:06,420
For a radio story, it would've been better if she fell and I had to help her, making me late.

102
00:19:06,420 --> 00:19:23,020
But I wasn't late. Hiyo arrived in a rush because she finished early, even though everyone had planned to meet later.

103
00:19:23,020 --> 00:19:39,780
She arrived while only half the staff were there, then called everyone who came after her “late.”

104
00:19:39,780 --> 00:19:54,060
“Are you an empress? Three late arrivals and you're off the show!”

105
00:19:54,060 --> 00:20:10,460
Everyone had planned to arrive later because Hiyo said she might be late. “It was a trap!”

106
00:20:10,460 --> 00:20:24,060
She told everyone the day before to take their time, then messaged, “I'm done, I'm on my way!”

107
00:20:24,060 --> 00:20:39,100
Yama-chan had even arrived early, but Hiyo still said, “You're late! Let's start!”

108
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:53,580
He answered with a sad little “brrr.” “Was Yama-chan a bird?”

109
00:20:54,640 --> 00:21:11,220
I know Yama-chan likes Nogizaka, so we've probably talked before. But I still don't know his voice.

110
00:21:11,220 --> 00:21:28,220
“Was that a little trill?” “Yama-chan's cute.” “I'm not cute!”

111
00:21:28,220 --> 00:21:42,220
That little sound reminded me of something. Hiyo, do you still do the “hororo” laugh?

112
00:21:42,220 --> 00:21:58,220
Everyone knows about it. You might be sensitive about it, so you don't have to answer if you don't want to.

113
00:21:58,220 --> 00:22:15,220
“Do you still laugh like ‘hororo’?” “We know that laugh.” “Maybe you didn't realize you did it.”

114
00:22:15,220 --> 00:22:34,660
I used to have a radio show on another station—Radio Kansai. “There, the culprit's been named!”

115
00:22:34,660 --> 00:22:53,080
A listener wrote that my laugh sounded like “hororo.” I listened back and realized I really was doing it.

116
00:22:53,080 --> 00:23:10,320
I love that laugh. I really do say “hororo.”

117
00:23:10,320 --> 00:23:24,320
It's become Hiyo's signature laugh. But lately, I haven't heard it.

118
00:23:24,320 --> 00:23:43,000
“Maybe people just don't notice it.” “I'm a hororo fan.” “Don't say that like you're a fan club!”

119
00:23:43,000 --> 00:23:57,660
When we went out to eat, I watched for ten years to see if the “hororo” would come out.

120
00:23:57,660 --> 00:24:14,020
But it hasn't happened in a decade. Maybe she heard about it on that radio show and consciously stopped.

121
00:24:14,020 --> 00:24:32,360
“If it was unconscious, you couldn't just fix it.” “Ordinary people don't laugh ‘hororo,’ no matter how hard they try.”

122
00:24:32,360 --> 00:24:49,300
I tried imitating it: “Ahaha… hororo.” It doesn't sound natural. You do it naturally while laughing.

123
00:24:49,300 --> 00:25:04,260
Maybe she trained her abs and learned to laugh differently. “I have a theory about ‘hororo.’”

124
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:20,840
Maybe “arara” gradually turned into “hororo.” “That's a stretch.” “Really?”

125
00:25:39,400 --> 00:25:57,860
That's all for “Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions.” It's time to wrap up. What happened?

126
00:25:57,860 --> 00:26:16,500
Saku, what's wrong? “The channel…” “Go ahead and explain it.”

127
00:26:16,500 --> 00:26:27,080
We'd love to hear from listeners. Send postcards to Bunka Hōsō or email the show.

128
00:26:27,080 --> 00:26:39,900
What happened was, while we weren't recording, I looked at the video and said, “My eyes aren't open today.”

129
00:26:39,900 --> 00:26:53,440
I rubbed my eyes like this, and Saku thought I was a monster pulling its eyes out. “That was the bit, right?”

130
00:26:53,460 --> 00:27:04,900
“No, the line was ‘My eyes won't open today.’” “I was saying ‘Go! Go!’”

131
00:27:04,900 --> 00:27:18,900
When your eyes get tired late at night, you pep yourself up to open them. But nobody says “Go!” while doing that.

132
00:27:18,900 --> 00:27:28,900
“That's like a Beyblade launch!” “Go! Go! Go!”

133
00:27:29,900 --> 00:27:42,520
Try it and report back. The show airs Tuesdays at 1 a.m. on Bunka Hōsō.

134
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:56,200
The video version comes out Thursday at 9:30 p.m., and the audio archive is on Onsen at 10 p.m.

135
00:27:56,200 --> 00:28:10,620
There's also a YouTube channel, with video archives and after-talks for members. “Has membership started?” “Not yet!”

136
00:28:10,620 --> 00:28:24,220
Please subscribe to the YouTube channel. “What do you do when your eyes get blurry?” “I tell myself, ‘All right, let's go!’”

137
00:28:24,220 --> 00:28:35,400
“You don't say ‘Go, go!’” “Are we recording at one in the morning? This feels like a third after-party.”

138
00:28:35,400 --> 00:28:46,380
“Let's wrap up.” “This was Yoko Hikasa and Ayane Sakura. Bye-bye!”

139
00:28:46,380 --> 00:29:13,720
“Hikasa and Sakura Won't Allow Digressions”—the YouTube after-talk. The show airs Tuesday night, with the video and audio archives on Thursday.

140
00:29:13,720 --> 00:29:27,520
We talked so much that we didn't read any listener mail. Some shows never get to their mail, right?

141
00:29:27,520 --> 00:29:40,500
Let's try not to do that. First letter: “Parasite Fat.” “Parasitic weight gain.”

142
00:29:40,500 --> 00:29:58,640
“What was ‘mamenme’ again?” “You mean ‘majime’—serious? We talked about that last week.”

143
00:29:58,640 --> 00:30:16,260
“The first broadcast? NHK Radio 1?” “No, the first episode!”

144
00:30:16,260 --> 00:30:35,560
“I watched the first episode on YouTube in high quality. You both looked lovely, even without hair and makeup.”

145
00:30:35,560 --> 00:30:53,800
The letter asks: when did you start calling each other Hiyo and Saku, and how did you become close?

146
00:30:53,800 --> 00:31:12,340
We got close pretty soon after Saku joined the agency. Hiyo gets along with almost all her juniors.

147
00:31:12,340 --> 00:31:30,880
“You don't have to hide the agency name. I left on good terms.” “Sure, but it's another company.”

148
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:50,620
There are so many juniors that sometimes I meet the same person ten times and still don't remember their name or face.

149
00:31:50,620 --> 00:32:10,260
But whenever the two of us met, we laughed nonstop—even right up until I left the agency.

150
00:32:10,260 --> 00:32:27,580
We'd eat pizza and laugh so loudly that they removed the space we used. The desk staff couldn't concentrate.

151
00:32:27,580 --> 00:32:47,420
We'd eat, hang around, talk, laugh, and bother people. We even parked in the lot. “You were the only one who got chased off.”

152
00:32:47,420 --> 00:33:03,200
“Saku was told she could park there anytime.” “They were just playing favorites with us!”

153
00:33:03,200 --> 00:33:20,180
“Maybe it was your attitude.” “I could've asked more sweetly.” “You really are cute, Hiyo.”

154
00:33:20,180 --> 00:33:38,860
“That opened a door we didn't need to open.” “I'm not just a fun, cheerful senior. I liked that you weren't vulgar.”

155
00:33:38,860 --> 00:33:58,280
“You said ‘I liked’ in the past tense!” “I mean, I like you.” “Who has the kind of charm you admire?” “Kikuko-san.”

156
00:33:58,280 --> 00:34:16,580
“I knew it! She's always so adorable.” “Once she showed me a little acorn and said, ‘Look, Hiyo-chan!’”

157
00:34:16,580 --> 00:34:35,440
“Does she live in Animal Crossing? That doesn't happen in real life.” “It does—there are acorns in Tokyo.”

158
00:34:35,440 --> 00:34:54,320
“Your acorn story isn't cute.” “It's cute when Kikuko-san says it.” “If I did it, you'd tell me to throw it away.”

159
00:34:54,320 --> 00:35:13,700
“You'd say bugs would crawl out of it.” “Maybe that's what charm is: the same thing feels different depending on who does it.”

160
00:35:13,700 --> 00:35:30,840
If Hiyo were too adorable, I might've kept my distance, thinking I shouldn't get too close.

161
00:35:30,840 --> 00:35:49,780
“Saku-chan, acorns have bugs, so boil them first.” “That sounds so distant.”

162
00:35:49,780 --> 00:36:05,360
“Why did you choose me out of all your juniors?” “You were an interesting woman. It wasn't your charm.”

163
00:36:05,360 --> 00:36:23,220
“What about my charm?” “Saku always treated me like her senior, even while teasing me.”

164
00:36:23,220 --> 00:36:40,220
She'd joke around, but when I brought food, she'd always say thank you and thank me for the meal.

165
00:36:40,220 --> 00:36:57,060
She knew where the line was and was always careful with me. I thought she was a wonderful person.

166
00:36:57,060 --> 00:37:15,300
Because Hiyo is kind, some juniors can take her for granted. I hated seeing that.

167
00:37:15,300 --> 00:37:33,700
Sometimes I worry I teased her too much, and even now I occasionally think I went too far.

168
00:37:33,700 --> 00:37:53,840
“But sometimes Saku stands up for me.” “Do I?” “You say, ‘Don't bully Hiyo!’”

169
00:37:53,840 --> 00:38:11,080
She's my guardian. “Guardian Saku!” “That's one of the things I love about you.”

170
00:38:11,080 --> 00:38:30,080
That's how we became close, and now we're doing a radio show together. “It's embarrassing.”

171
00:38:30,080 --> 00:38:46,600
“I thought we'd read three letters.” “Should we read one more?” “Isn't the after-talk a paid bonus?”

172
00:38:46,600 --> 00:39:08,360
A listener says the YouTube picture was much clearer than A&G, and they could finally see Hiyo's octopus impression.

173
00:39:08,360 --> 00:39:26,360
“What other sea-creature impressions do you do?” “I never said I was good at those!” “Why did you do an octopus?”

174
00:39:26,360 --> 00:39:43,860
The main show airs on terrestrial radio, so visual gags don't come across. In the after-talk, you can go all out.

175
00:39:43,860 --> 00:40:02,520
“Maybe we should face each other like a radio show.” “Let's try it next week.” “Hima-chan, you're in charge of that segment.”

176
00:40:02,520 --> 00:40:20,020
“Try saying ‘hororo.’” “We could put a table between us and turn our chairs around.”

177
00:40:20,020 --> 00:40:39,260
“What's wrong?” “I looked over and thought, ‘My eyes aren't open.’” “They're more open than you think.”

178
00:40:39,260 --> 00:40:58,560
“Are they sunken?” “No, no.” “Now I feel self-conscious. This is the problem with being on camera.”

179
00:40:58,560 --> 00:41:13,180
The main show will eventually be on YouTube too. “Now I'm discouraged. Maybe I'll quit this show.”

